# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this template is not.
#
# Stages 1-5 and 7 need nothing here. Only the Stage 6 poller requires a value.

# Optional path overrides, if the export lives outside the project directory.
# SPOTIFY_EXPORT_DIR=/path/to/Spotify Extended Streaming History
# SPOTIFY_DATA_DIR=/path/to/data
# SPOTIFY_CACHE_DIR=/path/to/.cache
# SPOTIFY_OUTPUT_DIR=/path/to/output

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage 6 poller (poll.py) — the ONE thing that still needs a Spotify app.
#
# Reading your own recently-played is user-scoped, so there is no way around
# authenticating as yourself. Auth is Authorization Code with PKCE, which means
# no client secret is ever used or stored — the client ID alone is enough.
#
# In the Spotify developer dashboard, set the app's redirect URI to exactly:
#     http://127.0.0.1:3000
# It must match character for character, including the port and the absence of
# a trailing slash, or the callback is rejected.
#
# The access token is cached in .cache/spotify_token.json (gitignored).
# `poll.py --logout` deletes it.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET is NOT used anywhere and should not be set.
#
# The original design also called for Spotify's artist endpoint as a genre
# fallback, because it used to return a `genres` array. It does not any more:
# /v1/artists/{id} now answers 200 with `genres`, `popularity` and `followers`
# all absent (checked against Drake, Taylor Swift, Metallica and ArrDee), batch
# /v1/artists and /top-tracks answer 403, and search never carried genres.
#
# Untagged artists are backfilled from MusicBrainz release-group tags instead,
# which needs no credentials at all.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
